          "The second classification is At-Least-Once delivery: messages are guaranteed to never be lost, but may occasionally be delivered and processed multiple times. Performance benchmarks should be run on realistic data sizes and hardware configurations, not just on small toy examples that fit entirely in cache and give misleadingly fast results.",
          "The third classification is Exactly-Once delivery: every message is guaranteed to effect state updates exactly once, with zero loss and zero duplicates. Logging every significant state transition in this component helps during post-incident analysis when you need to reconstruct what happened in the minutes before a failure.",
          "While exactly-once sounds universally superior, it requires end-to-end transactional coordination that introduces computational overhead. Looking deeper, the mathematical properties of this approach guarantee certain performance bounds that hold even under adversarial input patterns, which is critical for security-sensitive applications.",
          "The vast majority of enterprise streaming architectures default to at-least-once delivery as their foundational baseline. The historical evolution of this technique reveals how the industry gradually identified and addressed weaknesses in earlier approaches through empirical observation and theoretical analysis.",
          "Under at-least-once delivery, the streaming broker guarantees durability, while downstream consumers design for idempotence to neutralize duplicates. Comparing this method against its main alternatives on the dimensions of latency, throughput, memory usage, and implementation complexity shows that no single approach dominates on all fronts.",
