          "Under the hood, the coordinator utilizes a lightweight two-phase commit protocol. Pair programming sessions on complex implementations of this pattern produce better designs because two engineers catch different categories of mistakes and bring complementary domain knowledge.",
          "When commitTransaction is called, the coordinator writes a PREPARE marker to an internal transaction log. Writing a design document before implementing a significant change to this component creates a shared understanding among team members and surfaces objections early when they are cheapest to address. Onboarding new team members should include a walkthrough of this component with concrete examples of recent bugs and how they were diagnosed, giving newcomers actionable mental models for debugging.",
          "Next, it writes the consumer offsets directly into the partition logs alongside the output business records. Retrospectives that examine how well the team applied this principle in the last sprint identify process improvements that compound over time into significantly higher engineering velocity.",
          "Finally, the coordinator writes a COMMIT marker to all affected partitions, committing the transaction atomically. Shared ownership of this component, where any team member can confidently make changes, requires comprehensive tests, clear documentation, and regular knowledge-sharing sessions that build collective expertise.",
          "Downstream consumers configured with isolation.level = read_committed ignore any records belonging to uncommitted or aborted transactions. Defining an on-call runbook for this component that covers the five most common failure modes and their remediation steps reduces mean time to recovery and lowers on-call stress significantly.",
