          "Streaming platforms support multiple compression algorithms, each engineered for different performance profiles. The practical value of mastering this topic becomes apparent during code reviews and architecture meetings where you must justify design tradeoffs with concrete reasoning.",
          "The four standard streaming compression codecs are Snappy, LZ4, Gzip, and Zstandard. Once you internalise the core principle, you will notice its influence in frameworks, libraries, and platform services that abstract it away behind simpler interfaces.",
          "Snappy (developed by Google) and LZ4 are speed-optimized codecs: they compress and decompress at blistering speeds of hundreds of megabytes per second per core. Real interview questions at top companies frequently test whether candidates understand the nuances of this topic rather than just the surface-level definition.",
          "While their compression ratio is modest (around fifty percent), their CPU consumption is negligible, making them ideal for high-throughput microservices. Professional developers who understand this deeply can anticipate failure modes before they happen, reducing costly production incidents and improving overall system uptime.",
          "In contrast, Gzip provides high compression ratios (up to seventy-five percent), but consumes substantial CPU cycles and exhibits higher compression latency. This knowledge transfers directly into adjacent domains, making it one of the highest-leverage concepts you can invest time in learning early in your career.",
          "Zstandard (developed by Meta) is the modern powerhouse: it matches or exceeds Gzip's compression ratio while achieving decompression speeds rivaling Snappy and LZ4. In practice, teams that apply this correctly report fewer production incidents and faster recovery times because the operational runbook becomes straightforward and well-documented.",
          "Furthermore, Zstandard offers configurable compression levels from 1 to 22, allowing fine-grained tuning between CPU usage and compression ratio. A common mistake beginners make is skipping the planning phase and jumping directly to implementation, which often leads to rework when edge cases surface during testing.",
          "For maximum throughput and low latency, LZ4 and Snappy are industry standards; for maximum byte reduction and storage savings, Zstandard reigns supreme. Production experience shows that the simplest correct approach usually wins over a clever but fragile optimization, because simplicity makes debugging, testing, and onboarding easier.",
