        "title": "State Accumulation: Count, Sum, Min, Max & Online Averages",
        "say": [
          "Once events are mapped to their respective window boundaries, the stream processor must accumulate state over time. Real interview questions at top companies frequently test whether candidates understand the nuances of this topic rather than just the surface-level definition. Professional developers who understand this deeply can anticipate failure modes before they happen, reducing costly production incidents and improving overall system uptime.",
          "A naive implementation might store every raw event in an array until the window closes, then calculate statistics at the end. This knowledge transfers directly into adjacent domains, making it one of the highest-leverage concepts you can invest time in learning early in your career.",
          "However, storing raw events in memory requires memory proportional to event volume, leading to buffer exhaustion under high traffic. In practice, teams that apply this correctly report fewer production incidents and faster recovery times because the operational runbook becomes straightforward and well-documented.",
          "Efficient stream processors use online accumulation: updating running summary statistics incrementally as each event arrives. A common mistake beginners make is skipping the planning phase and jumping directly to implementation, which often leads to rework when edge cases surface during testing.",
          "For basic aggregations, an accumulator requires only a few primitive numbers: count, running sum, running min, and running max. Production experience shows that the simplest correct approach usually wins over a clever but fragile optimization, because simplicity makes debugging, testing, and onboarding easier.",
          "Online average is calculated simply by dividing the running sum by the running count at any point during window execution. When implementing this in a real project, start with the simplest version that handles the common case correctly, then add complexity only after profiling reveals a genuine bottleneck.",
          "Online accumulation achieves O(1) constant memory per active window regardless of whether ten or ten million events are processed. Automated tests for this kind of logic should cover not just the happy path but also boundary conditions, error handling, and concurrent access scenarios that are common in production.",
          "This dramatic memory savings allows a single Node.js process to maintain thousands of active analytical windows concurrently. Monitoring and observability are essential companions to this technique because they let you verify at runtime that the system is behaving as designed rather than relying on hope.",
          "Designing lightweight online accumulators is a prerequisite for building production-grade high-throughput stream aggregators. Code that implements this pattern should be isolated behind a clean interface so that future improvements or replacements do not require changes throughout the entire codebase."
        ],
        "example": "A pedometer counting steps throughout the day; it increments an integer counter rather than storing a GPS coordinate for every footstep.",
        "code": "interface WindowAccumulator {\n  windowStart: number;\n  windowEnd: number;\n  count: number;\n  sum: number;\n  min: number;\n  max: number;\n  get average(): number;\n}\n\nclass TumblingAggregator {\n  private windows = new Map<number, WindowAccumulator>();\n\n  constructor(private windowDurationMs: number) {}\n\n  addEvent(timestamp: number, value: number): void {\n    const start = timestamp - (timestamp % this.windowDurationMs);\n    let acc = this.windows.get(start);\n\n    if (!acc) {\n      acc = {\n        windowStart: start,\n        windowEnd: start + this.windowDurationMs,\n        count: 0,\n        sum: 0,\n        min: Infinity,\n        max: -Infinity,\n        get average() { return this.count === 0 ? 0 : Number((this.sum / this.count).toFixed(2)); }\n      };\n      this.windows.set(start, acc);\n    }\n\n    acc.count++;\n    acc.sum += value;\n    acc.min = Math.min(acc.min, value);\n    acc.max = Math.max(acc.max, value);\n  }\n\n  getWindows(): WindowAccumulator[] {\n    return Array.from(this.windows.values()).sort((a, b) => a.windowStart - b.windowStart);\n  }\n}\n\nconst agg = new TumblingAggregator(1000);\nagg.addEvent(1050, 10);\nagg.addEvent(1200, 30);\nagg.addEvent(1800, 20);\nagg.addEvent(2100, 100);\n\nagg.getWindows().forEach(w => {\n  console.log(`Window ${w.windowStart}-${w.windowEnd}: Count=${w.count}, Sum=${w.sum}, Avg=${w.average}, Min=${w.min}, Max=${w.max}`);\n});",
