          "The core governing parameter of every session window system is the Inactivity Gap Threshold (gapMs). Shared ownership of this component, where any team member can confidently make changes, requires comprehensive tests, clear documentation, and regular knowledge-sharing sessions that build collective expertise. Defining an on-call runbook for this component that covers the five most common failure modes and their remediation steps reduces mean time to recovery and lowers on-call stress significantly.",
          "The inactivity gap defines the maximum allowable duration of silence between two consecutive events before a session divides. Cross-team dependencies on this component should be managed through versioned interfaces and change notifications so that upstream changes do not silently break downstream consumers or introduce regressions.",
          "If event B arrives within gapMs of event A, event B extends the current session's end boundary to event B's timestamp. Celebrating when the team catches a potential issue in this area during code review reinforces the culture of careful engineering and encourages thorough reviews in future pull requests.",
          "Conversely, if the elapsed time between event A and event B strictly exceeds gapMs, event A's session terminates. Technical debt in this component should be tracked explicitly and prioritised alongside feature work, because deferred maintenance eventually manifests as production incidents or development slowdowns that cost more.",
          "Event B then initializes a brand new, independent session window with start and end equal to event B's timestamp. Understanding this concept thoroughly helps you reason about why certain design choices outperform others in real-world production environments where reliability and speed both matter.",
          "Selecting an appropriate gap threshold depends on the business domain: e-commerce often uses 30 minutes, while gaming uses 5 minutes. When you grasp the underlying mechanics, you can predict how the system will behave under unusual conditions such as sudden traffic spikes or partial hardware failures.",
          "Too short a gap threshold fragments a continuous user journey into dozens of artificially small micro-sessions. The intuition behind this idea extends far beyond textbook examples because production systems combine multiple interacting components that amplify small inefficiencies into serious bottlenecks.",
