        "Recovery procedures for validateDeadGuard are designed to be idempotent — running the recovery process multiple times produces the same result as running it once. This property is essential for automated recovery, where a monitoring system might trigger the same recovery action repeatedly before detecting that the first invocation succeeded. Idempotency is enforced through unique operation identifiers and conditional writes that check for prior completion.",
        "Testing resilience in Dead-Letter Queue (DLQ) Exponential Replay Tiers & Poison-Pill Quarantine goes beyond unit tests. The implementation includes chaos engineering hooks that allow operators to inject specific failures — increased latency, dropped connections, corrupted data — in a controlled manner. These hooks are gated behind a configuration flag and are used in pre-production environments to validate that the system behaves correctly under adverse conditions before those conditions arise in production.",
        "The warm standby architecture in this function maintains a continuously updated replica that can take over processing within seconds of a primary failure. The replica receives a real-time stream of state updates from the primary and applies them locally. When the primary fails, the replica completes any in-flight state updates and begins accepting new traffic, providing near-zero recovery time."
