        "Graceful degradation in Milestone 11 means the system continues to provide value even when some components are unavailable. The validateCepGuard implementation defines a hierarchy of fallback strategies: first try the primary path, then fall back to a cached result, then fall back to a default value, and finally return a typed error that explains exactly what is degraded and why. Each fallback level is logged and metered so the operations team knows the system's current capability level.",
        "Recovery procedures for validateCepGuard are designed to be idempotent — running the recovery process multiple times produces the same result as running it once. This property is essential for automated recovery, where a monitoring system might trigger the same recovery action repeatedly before detecting that the first invocation succeeded. Idempotency is enforced through unique operation identifiers and conditional writes that check for prior completion.",
        "Testing resilience in Milestone 11 goes beyond unit tests. The implementation includes chaos engineering hooks that allow operators to inject specific failures — increased latency, dropped connections, corrupted data — in a controlled manner. These hooks are gated behind a configuration flag and are used in pre-production environments to validate that the system behaves correctly under adverse conditions before those conditions arise in production.",
